---
import CaseStudy from '../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';

const facts = [
  { k: 'City', v: 'Vienna' },
  { k: 'Year', v: '2019' },
  { k: 'Structure', v: 'Timber hybrid' },
  { k: 'Architect', v: 'einszueins architektur' },
  { k: 'Tenure', v: 'Baugruppe' },
  { k: 'District', v: 'Sonnwendviertel' },
];

const gallery = [
  {
    placeholder: 'Timber facade — photo needed',
    cap: 'The timber-hybrid house from the street',
    src: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sonnwendviertel%20Gleis%2021.jpg?width=1400',
    credit: 'Linie29 · CC BY-SA 4.0',
    creditHref: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sonnwendviertel_Gleis_21.jpg',
  },
  {
    placeholder: 'Ground floor — photo needed',
    cap: 'The ground floor on Bloch-Bauer-Promenade — public bookcase, open to the quarter',
    src: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gleis%2021%20-%20public%20bookcase.jpg?width=1400',
    credit: 'Herzi Pinki · CC BY-SA 4.0',
    creditHref: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gleis_21_-_public_bookcase.jpg',
  },
  { placeholder: 'Roof terrace — photo needed', cap: 'The shared roof' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Gleis 21"
  subtitle="Vienna's building-group culture, in current form."
  heroSrc="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sonnwendviertel%20Gleis%2021.jpg?width=1400"
  heroPlaceholder="Gleis 21 hero — timber facade (photo needed)"
  heroCredit="Linie29 · CC BY-SA 4.0"
  heroCreditHref="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sonnwendviertel_Gleis_21.jpg"
  facts={facts}
  gallery={gallery}
  prev={{ href: '/hunziker-areal/', label: 'Hunziker Areal' }}
  next={{ href: '/la-balma/', label: 'La Balma' }}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-6 pt-16 sm:px-12">
    <p class="text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      In the Sonnwendviertel — the new quarter behind Vienna's main station — a building group
      raised a timber-hybrid house whose ground floor belongs half to the city: a media workshop, an
      event room, a sauna, a library, all programmed for the neighborhood as much as for the
      residents upstairs.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      What to steal: how it got the land. Vienna allocates sites in new districts partly by concept
      competition — the best program wins, not the highest bid. A Berkeley disposition of public
      land could copy that rule tomorrow.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="lessons">
    <li>Award public land by concept, not price — the single most copyable rule in this atlas.</li>
    <li>Timber construction and a Baugruppe fit together: light, fast, precut, self-finished.</li>
    <li>Program the ground floor for the quarter and the quarter will defend the building.</li>
    <li>New districts need groups seeded early, while land is still cheap on paper.</li>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="sources">
    Sources: <a href="https://gleis21.wien" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gleis21.wien</a>
  </Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
